{"title": "Investigate: Focused spreadsheet × Candidate B", "question": "Measure duration of focused attention and ask whether participants distinguish it from immersion.", "search_plan": "Search scholarly indexes and the university library for "immersion sustained attention absorption", "narrative engagement attention" and "task concentration presence". Prioritize studies distinguishing attention duration from immersion or absorption with separate measures. Record original results and competing explanations; do not treat the hypothetical case as an observation.

Executed 2026-10-09: web searches "Jennett 2008 measuring defining experience immersion games task attention immersion study" and "Agarwal Karahanna 2000 cognitive absorption focused immersion software technology temporal dissociation". Inspected Jennett author manuscript sections on Experiment 2, questionnaire refinement and general discussion; inspected the original MISQ/AIS abstract. Retained these two primary sources for directly relevant attention/immersion arguments. Other search hits were not used as findings. This was a targeted pilot search, not a systematic review or exhaustive spreadsheet search.", "owner": "Assistant — delegated review", "priority": "normal", "status": "investigating", "findings_summary": "Bounded evidence review completed. Analyst inference: sustained attention is a plausible component but an insufficiently discriminating standalone definition of immersiveness. Simple-task immersion-like reports also caution against excluding spreadsheet work solely because it lacks narrative transportation. Neither source directly tests the spreadsheet scenario. The existing included judgment remains valid only under Candidate B V1 wording, not as empirical confirmation of immersion.", "decision": "revise definition", "resolution": "Recommendation, not an applied definition change: retain Candidate B V1 as the attentional-engagement comparator. Develop a separate revised immersion-state candidate incorporating experiential involvement and test its distinctiveness from attention and cognitive absorption. Do not yet select a necessary additional dimension. Compare spreadsheet work with a narrative/game task matched for attention and workload, measuring attention, dissociation, involvement and reported immersion separately. Test measurement distinctiveness and explanatory value. Keep this task investigating until direct evidence addresses the spreadsheet question; no construct approval or saturation claim.

Prepared candidate: Candidate B2 — experiential immersion (proposed state) (record 082cc490-c289-4706-b698-bd6ed73de903, version 1). Its evidence links retain the two inspected findings. A separate B-versus-B2 test and historical comparison are saved. Original B and all classifications remain unchanged. B2 concerns experienced immersion; environmental immersiveness requires a separate capacity definition. Task remains investigating."}

